# The durable key and session key have separate jobs

Each endpoint has a durable Ed25519 keypair. Its public key has two related
roles:

- It is the **iroh node id** the transport authenticates the QUIC channel against.
- It is the **designator**, the peer's name inside an OCapN locator, the part
  you dial in an `ocapn://<node-id>.iroh` URI.

Every CapTP session also draws a fresh signing key. The hello signature proves
control of that session key and binds it to the locator the partner claims. It
does not prove control of the durable node id. The iroh channel proves that,
and glowcap requires the hello's claimed designator to match the id the channel
authenticated.

Binding from the same seed restores the node identity at a new network
address. A sturdyref works after that restart only if the application also
restores the named object under the same swiss number.

# Reaching a peer

A peer hands you its node id. You choose how that id becomes a route when you
bind the endpoint:

| Mode | How a dial resolves |
| --- | --- |
| `direct` | Locator hints carry the peer's socket addresses |
| `local_discovery` | An in-process address book resolves the id |
| `discovery` | Public n0 relays and DNS/pkarr resolve the id |
| `discovery_no_relay` | DNS/pkarr resolves published addresses with relay fallback disabled |

`direct` and `local_discovery` are deterministic. `discovery` publishes node
information and depends on public infrastructure and propagation. The
no-relay form needs a publicly reachable direct address.

# Optional Rust library

iroh's QUIC stack is Rust, so glowcap ships it as a separate static library,
`libglowcap_net_transport_iroh`
([ADR-0055](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0055-iroh-netlayer-sidecar.md)).
The base archives stay pure Zig, and building this one requires `cargo`. Every
language keeps it optional:

- A Zig build imports `glowcap.module("glowcap_iroh")`, which links the
  Rust library. A program that never imports it never runs cargo.
- Each binding keeps iroh in its own module: the `@glowcap/iroh` package,
  `glowcap.iroh`, `glowcap-iroh.rkt`, and Rust's opt-in `iroh` feature.
- `connect` loads that module the first time it sees an `iroh` locator, so a
  program that only speaks TCP never loads it. Rust compiles it in behind the
  feature instead.

Otherwise `listen` and `connect` work as they do on TCP. Only the address
differs.

# Scale: many connections, one thread

The blocking path uses one OS thread per connection. To serve many, a driver loop
drives every connection from one readiness descriptor
([ADR-0057](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0057-iroh-completion-loop-bridge.md)).
[Performance]($link.page('performance')) measures the serial and readiness-loop
paths. iroh's memory is separate from glowcap's core session state.

# Three-party handoff over iroh

A [handoff]($link.page('concepts')) over iroh works as on any session: the
gifter names the exporter by node id, and the recipient redeems by dialing that
id on a fresh encrypted session.

For a full application, run the [rooms]($link.page('examples')) chat with
`zig build demo-rooms-iroh`. [What you can depend on]($link.page('stability'))
separates deterministic checks from public discovery tests.
